<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benedict of Nursia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 480–<a href="/years/547">547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Saint Benedict, Benedictus Nursiensis, Benedetto da Norcia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benedict of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norcia">Nursia</a>, often known as Saint Benedict, was an Italian monk regarded as the founder of the Order of Saint Benedict. The son of a Roman noble of Nursia, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to study but left it, and he founded twelve communities for monks at Subiaco before founding the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> in 530. His Rule of Saint Benedict, influenced by John Cassian and close to the earlier Rule of the Master, was adopted by most religious communities founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> and became one of the most influential in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Western Christendom</a>. The only ancient account of his life is in the Dialogues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>; according to tradition he died on 21 March 547.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benedict-of-nursia.jpg" alt="Benedict of Nursia" width="330" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fra Angelico · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fra_Angelico_031.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Apart from a short poem attributed to Mark of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a>, the only ancient account of Benedict is in the second book of the Dialogues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory I</a>, thought to have been written in 593, although the authenticity of the work is disputed. Gregory did not set out to write a biography but a spiritual portrait, and said his information came from four of Benedict's disciples, among them Constantinus, who succeeded him as abbot of Monte Cassino. According to this account Benedict was born around 480 and was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to study, but was disappointed by what he found. He left, probably a few years before 500, with his nurse and settled at Enfide in the Simbruini mountains.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Near Subiaco the monk Romanus gave Benedict the monastic habit, and by his advice Benedict lived three years as a hermit in a cave above a lake, Romanus bringing him food. When the abbot of a neighbouring monastery died, its community asked Benedict to take his place, but he knew their manners differed from his and the monks tried to poison him, so he went back to his cave. Gregory tells that a priest named Florentius sent him a loaf of poisoned bread, which a raven carried away. Disciples came to Subiaco, and Benedict founded twelve monasteries near it. About 530 he left and founded the monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> on a hilltop between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><p>He wrote his Rule for monks living together under an abbot. It has seventy-three short chapters: more than half describe obedience and humility, about a quarter regulate the work of God, and the rest tell how and by whom the monastery should be managed. Benedictine asceticism is known for its moderation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Benedict died of a fever at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-cassino">Monte Cassino</a> not long after his sister Scholastica and was buried in the same tomb; tradition gives the date as 21 March 547. The early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> have been called the Benedictine centuries, and his Rule was the foundational document of thousands of religious communities.</p><p>The present-day Order of Saint Benedict emerged later and is a confederation of autonomous congregations, not an order in the usual sense. Pope Paul VI named him patron protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1964, and in 1980 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> declared him co-patron with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a>. A medal bearing his image was first struck in 1880 and was approved by Pope <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benedict-xiv">Benedict XIV</a> in 1741 and 1742. The basilica built on his birthplace in the 1400s was devastated by the earthquake of 30 October 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benedict of Nursia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
